        <article class="wiki__body prose">
            <h1 class="page__title">Wiki</h1>
            <p>Mnemo links code, business documents, business rules and agent memories in a single graph, and gives it back to humans (this site) and to AI agents (MCP server). This page explains what each screen is for.</p>
            <p>The usual path: declare a project, ingest its code and documents, let the agents send learnings, review the proposals, then keep it tidy with gardening.</p>

            {% for page in pages %}
                <section id="{{ page.id }}" class="wiki__section">
                    <h2>{{ page.title }}</h2>
                    <p>{{ page.what }}</p>
                    <ul>{% for line in page.how %}<li>{{ line }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
                    {% if page.url %}<p><a href="{{ page.url }}">Open {{ page.title }}</a></p>{% else %}<p>This page opens from a project, in the sidebar.</p>{% endif %}
                </section>
            {% endfor %}

            <section id="agents" class="wiki__section">
                <h2>Agents and MCP</h2>
                <p>Agents (Claude Code, Antigravity, Cursor, Codex...) plug into the MCP server at <code>/mcp</code>. Each tool call is counted on the dashboard.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>list_projects</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>file_context</code>, <code>get_document</code>, <code>memory_block</code>: read the graph.</li>
                    <li><code>add_memory</code>: stores a memory directly. <code>learn</code>: sends a source from which the LLM extracts proposals to review.</li>
                    <li><code>import_document</code>: adds a Markdown document.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>The content returned to the agents comes from users or external tools: it is always marked as data, never as an instruction.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="plugin" class="wiki__section">
                <h2>Agent plugins (Claude Code &amp; Antigravity)</h2>
                <p>The repository holds native plugins for Claude Code (<code>claude-plugin/</code>) and Antigravity (<code>agy-plugin/</code>). They bring the MCP server, the project memory at the start of a session, the context of each file read and the sending of learnings at the end of a session (secrets masked, to review here afterwards).</p>

                <h3>Claude Code</h3>
                <p>In the project's <code>.claude/settings.json</code>, committed:</p>
                <pre><code>{
  "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
    "mnemo": { "source": { "source": "github", "repo": "quentinRogeret34/Mnemo" } }
  },
  "enabledPlugins": { "mnemo@mnemo": true },
  "env": {
    "MNEMO_URL": "{{ app.request.schemeAndHttpHost }}"
  }
}</code></pre>
                <p>In <code>.claude/settings.local.json</code>, not committed, the key created in <a href="{{ path('api_key_index') }}">API keys</a>:</p>
                <pre><code>{ "env": { "MNEMO_API_KEY": "gk_..." } }</code></pre>

                <h3>Antigravity (<code>agy</code>)</h3>
                <p>Install the plugin from GitHub or a local clone:</p>
                <pre><code>agy plugin install https://github.com/quentinRogeret34/Mnemo/tree/main/agy-plugin
# or from a local clone:
agy plugin install /path/to/the/repository/agy-plugin</code></pre>
                <p>Export the environment variables for the hooks (and for a remote or API-key protected server, declare the explicit URL and <code>Authorization</code> header in <code>~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json</code>, since <code>mcp_config.json</code> does not expand <code>${VAR}</code>):</p>
                <pre><code>export MNEMO_URL="{{ app.request.schemeAndHttpHost }}"
export MNEMO_API_KEY="gk_..."</code></pre>

                <ul>
                    <li>The project is recognised by the <code>origin</code> remote, otherwise by the working directory. Unknown, it is created at the first session and joins the groups of the key's owner; a key limited to some projects creates nothing.</li>
                    <li><code>MNEMO_PROJECT</code>, optional, forces the project slug instead of deriving it from the repository.</li>
                    <li><code>MNEMO_DISABLED=1</code> turns the hooks off, <code>MNEMO_CAPTURE=0</code> only turns off sending the transcripts.</li>
                    <li>The machine needs <code>python3</code>. For Claude Code from a local clone, the marketplace source can be <code>{ "source": "directory", "path": "/path/to/the/repository" }</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="deploiement" class="wiki__section">
                <h2>Deploying on a server</h2>
                <p>For a VPS (OVH, Contabo, Hetzner, Scaleway...) running Debian or Ubuntu. The repository provides the production image (<code>Dockerfile</code>, <code>prod</code> target) and <code>compose.prod.yaml</code>: application, worker and Postgres with pgvector. FrankenPHP gets the HTTPS certificate on its own.</p>

                <h3>1. Prepare the server</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>2 vCPUs and 4 GB of memory are enough. Install Docker with its Compose plugin (<a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Docker documentation</a>).</li>
                    <li>Create a DNS A (and AAAA) record from the domain to the server's address.</li>
                    <li>Open ports 22, 80 and 443 in the firewall of the host and of the server. Postgres is not published.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>2. Configure</h3>
                <pre><code>git clone git@github.com:quentinRogeret34/Mnemo.git mnemo &amp;&amp; cd mnemo
cp .env.prod.dist .env.prod.local
openssl rand -hex 32    # APP_SECRET
openssl rand -hex 24    # POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code></pre>
                <p>Fill in <code>.env.prod.local</code>: domain (<code>SERVER_NAME</code>), both secrets, the LLM keys, the connector tokens and the OAuth ids of the sign-in providers (see <a href="#utilisateurs">Users and groups</a>). Then the password of the fallback <code>admin</code> account:</p>
                <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local build
docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local run --rm --no-deps php bin/console security:hash-password</code></pre>
                <p>Paste the hash in <code>APP_ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH</code>, doubling each <code>$</code> (<code>$$</code>), otherwise Compose reads it as a variable.</p>

                <h3>3. Start</h3>
                <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local up -d
docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local exec php bin/console doctrine:migrations:migrate --no-interaction
docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local exec php bin/console app:llm:check</code></pre>
                <p>The site answers on <code>https://&lt;domain&gt;</code>, behind <code>/login</code>. The agents go through <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/api</code> with an API key.</p>

                <h3>4. Connect the projects</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Nothing to declare: each project is created at the first session of a machine, from its remote repository. To choose the slug and the name, declare it first: <code>bin/console app:project:create &lt;slug&gt; "&lt;Name&gt;" &lt;path&gt;</code>, and set <code>MNEMO_PROJECT</code> on the machines.</li>
                    <li>Create one key per machine in API keys, then install the <a href="#plugin">plugin</a> in each repository.</li>
                    <li>The code graph is built on the server: create a “GitHub (code and history)” or GitLab connector on the project, it clones the repository and runs graphify at each sync. Otherwise, build it on a machine (<code>graphify extract --code-only</code>), copy <code>graph.json</code> to the server and run <code>bin/console app:ingest:code &lt;slug&gt; /tmp/graph.json</code>.</li>
                    <li>Add a scheduled GitHub or GitLab connector for the history: the worker syncs it on its own.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>5. Update and back up</h3>
                <pre><code>git pull
docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local up -d --build
docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local exec php bin/console doctrine:migrations:migrate --no-interaction

docker compose -f docker/compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod.local exec -T database pg_dump -U app app | gzip &gt; mnemo-$(date +%F).sql.gz</code></pre>
                <p>To back up: this dump (graph, memories, vectors) and the <code>storage</code> volume (files of the uploaded documents). The <code>caddy_data</code> volume keeps the certificates.</p>
            </section>
        </article>
